   "answer": "No, CUDA local memory is **not** always on-chip.\n\nAccording to the lecture, local memory is thread-private in scope, but it **usually resides in device DRAM** and passes through the L1/L2 caches [S1, p. 46]. This means that while it is logically private to a thread, its physical location is typically off-chip (in DRAM), not on-chip.\n\n**Reasoning:**\n- The lecture explicitly states that local memory \"usually resides in device DRAM\" [S1, p. 46].\n- Device DRAM is an off-chip memory resource, as confirmed by the description of the memory hierarchy where requests that miss in L2 continue to a memory controller and then to DRAM [S2, p. 44].\n- Therefore, local memory is generally not on-chip; it is stored in DRAM and accessed via the cache hierarchy (L1/L2).\n\n**Citation:**\n- [S1] Page 46: \"Local memory is thread-private in scope but usually resides in device DRAM and passes through L1/L2.\"\n- [S2] Page 44: \"A request reaches device-wide L2 through the on-chip interconnect. An L2 miss continues to a memory controller.\"",
